-- CLAUSE COVERAGE for `JavaEval.ard`.
--
-- `JavaEval` has exactly one clause per `JavaAst` constructor -- 14 `JExpr` plus
-- 7 `JStmt` -- so counting constructor occurrences in the Java that `ToJava`
-- generates for a program counts the `JavaEval` clauses that program's
-- evaluation can reach. This module does that counting; `ProbeCorpus.ard`'s
-- `coverageReport` prints the table for the corpus.
--
-- WHY BOTHER. Adapted from K-Java's `--coverage-file` (Bogdănaș & Roșu, POPL
-- 2015): the KJS payoff was discovering that the ECMAScript conformance suite
-- MISSED rules, and tests written for the missed ones found bugs in V8, WebKit
-- and SpiderMonkey. It is also the mirror of the λ□-side constructor counting
-- that justified "no more corpora" (`tApp` 10375, `tConst` 5432, ...) -- that
-- count is likewise static occurrences in the corpus terms, so the two numbers
-- are comparable. Cheap, and it turns "the corpus covers the semantics" from an
-- assumption into a number. Expect it to find clauses nothing exercises; that
-- result is the point, not a failure.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT THIS NUMBER IS NOT. Read this before quoting it.
--
-- It is STATIC, so it is an UPPER BOUND on dynamic clause coverage: it says the
-- constructor is in the generated program, not that evaluation reached it. The
-- two differ in exactly one interesting place -- a `jSwitch`'s `default` block
-- holds the `jThrow` for "no matching branch", which `ToJava` emits for EVERY
-- switch and which a correct program never runs. So a nonzero `jThrow` here
-- means "the throw was compiled", not "the throw was taken".
--
-- Direction of the error, stated plainly: a ZERO is exact and trustworthy -- the
-- constructor is absent, so the clause certainly never runs. A NONZERO is an
-- upper bound. That asymmetry is the useful way round, because the zeroes are
-- what the count exists to find.
--
-- Making it dynamic would mean threading a hit-set through `evalExpr`/`execStmt`
-- and their whole mutual cycle, i.e. changing the type of the semantics to
-- measure it. Not worth it for the zeroes, which are what this is for. The
-- clauses known to be exercised ONLY by a hand-written assertion and never by a
-- corpus program are named in `ExampleJEval.ard`: the unmatched-tag default
-- throw, the bad cast, and the `vUninit` read (JLS 16).
-- ============================================================================
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE RESULT, as printed by `ProbeCorpus.coverageReport` (3m9s, 7 programs).
-- Recorded here because it is a handover number, and because reproducing it
-- needs the gitignored `Imported.*` modules.
--
--   clause          progs   occs        clause          progs   occs
--   jVar            2       73          jLocal          2       29
--   jStatic         2       8           jAssign         2       16
--   jField          3       16          jSwitch         2       6
--   jIndex          2       15          jThrow          2       6
--   jCast           5       72          jReturn         7       58
--   jInvoke         5       61          jExprStmt       7       7
--   jCallStatic     7       73          jComment        0       0
--   jNew            6       32
--   jNewArrayInit   6       26        nodes per program:
--   jNewArraySized  2       3           peano            78
--   jAnonFn         7       31          leanbench-unit   20
--   jStringLit      2       6           mangle           15
--   jIntLit         6       44          axiom-mangle     20
--   jLongLit        2       20          leanbench-demo0  34
--                                       peregrine-lean   35
--                                       leanbench-even  400
--
-- THREE FINDINGS, and the second is the one worth acting on.
--
-- 1. ONE clause is at zero: `jComment`. And it is not a corpus gap -- it is
--    UNREACHABLE. `ToJava.ard` contains no `jComment` at all (checked: zero
--    occurrences); the comment an erased inductive produces is a
--    `jMemberComment`, a class-body member, which `JavaEval` correctly has no
--    clause for. So no corpus can ever cover `JavaEval`'s `jComment` clause, and
--    adding programs will not change that. It exists because `JavaAst` offers
--    the constructor, and the honest status is "dead in the current backend".
--
-- 2. THE CORPUS IS MUCH NARROWER THAN SEVEN PROGRAMS SUGGESTS. Only TWO programs
--    -- peano and leanbench-even -- exercise `jVar`, `jIndex`, `jLocal`,
--    `jAssign`, `jSwitch`, `jThrow` and `jNewArraySized`. That is the whole of
--    control flow, local binding, pattern matching and the `fix` knot resting on
--    two programs. The other five (leanbench-unit, mangle, axiom-mangle,
--    leanbench-demo0, peregrine-lean) are 15-35 nodes of essentially nothing but
--    constructor building: they hit `jCallStatic`, `jNew`, `jNewArrayInit`,
--    `jAnonFn`, `jReturn`, `jExprStmt` and stop. Five programs at 100% pass rate
--    reads as much better coverage than it is -- the same lesson `f040517`
--    recorded when six hand-built instances found nothing and one real program
--    found an interface defect.
--
-- 3. ONE PROGRAM IS TWO THIRDS OF THE CORPUS: leanbench-even is 400 of the 602
--    total nodes, and is the sole source of most of the `jVar`/`jCast`/`jInvoke`
--    counts. It is also the only program contributing `jLongLit` beyond peano's
--    zero, i.e. nearly all primitive-integer coverage.
--
-- What this says about where to spend corpus effort: not more Lean benchmarks
-- that build constructors, but programs with matching and recursion. It does NOT
-- say the semantics is wrong anywhere -- coverage is not correctness.
-- ============================================================================

\import JavaAst
\import JavaValue
\import LambdaBox
\import ToJava
\import StringUtil
-- Imported selectively, as `JavaPrint.ard` and `ToJava.ard` do: an unrestricted
-- `\import Data.Array` shadows `String`'s `++`.
\import Data.Array (map, mkArray)
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String

-- 14 `JExpr` constructors then 7 `JStmt` ones, in `JavaAst.ard` declaration
-- order. The index IS the clause identity, so `clauseName` and the `countExpr` /
-- `countStmt` clauses below must stay in step.
\func nClauses : Nat => 21

\func clauseName (i : Nat) : String
  | 0 => "jVar" | 1 => "jStatic" | 2 => "jField" | 3 => "jIndex"
  | 4 => "jCast" | 5 => "jInvoke" | 6 => "jCallStatic" | 7 => "jNew"
  | 8 => "jNewArrayInit" | 9 => "jNewArraySized" | 10 => "jAnonFn"
  | 11 => "jStringLit" | 12 => "jIntLit" | 13 => "jLongLit"
  | 14 => "jLocal" | 15 => "jAssign" | 16 => "jSwitch" | 17 => "jThrow"
  | 18 => "jReturn" | 19 => "jExprStmt" | 20 => "jComment"
  | _ => "?"

\func zeroCounts : Array Nat => replicateArr nClauses 0

-- A single occurrence of clause `i`.
\func one (i : Nat) : Array Nat => setArr i 1 zeroCounts

-- Pointwise. The `nil` clauses make this total; every vector here has length
-- `nClauses`, so they are never taken with a nonempty other side.
\func addCounts (a b : Array Nat) : Array Nat \elim a, b
  | nil, bs => bs
  | as, nil => as
  | a :: as, b :: bs => (a Nat.+ b) :: addCounts as bs

\func sumCounts (xs : Array (Array Nat)) : Array Nat \elim xs
  | nil => zeroCounts
  | x :: xs => addCounts x (sumCounts xs)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The counters.
--
-- Sub-arrays are traversed as `mkArray ... (\lam i => count... (xs i))` rather
-- than with `map`, which is the discipline `ToJava.ard:22-38` and
-- `JavaPrint.ard:65-79` document: applying the array to an index keeps the
-- structural link the termination checker needs across the `countExpr` /
-- `countStmt` mutual cycle. `map` breaks it.
--
-- `jLocal`'s `Maybe` is matched by NESTED patterns for the same reason -- an
-- inner `\case` on the initialiser would lose that link (`JavaPrint.ard:87-89`).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func countExpr (e : JExpr) : Array Nat \elim e
  | jVar _ => one 0
  | jStatic _ => one 1
  | jField e _ => addCounts (one 2) (countExpr e)
  | jIndex a i => addCounts (one 3) (addCounts (countExpr a) (countExpr i))
  | jCast _ e => addCounts (one 4) (countExpr e)
  | jInvoke e _ args =>
    addCounts (one 5) (addCounts (countExpr e)
      (sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {args.len} (\lam i => countExpr (args i)))))
  | jCallStatic _ args =>
    addCounts (one 6) (sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {args.len} (\lam i => countExpr (args i))))
  | jNew _ args =>
    addCounts (one 7) (sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {args.len} (\lam i => countExpr (args i))))
  | jNewArrayInit _ es =>
    addCounts (one 8) (sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {es.len} (\lam i => countExpr (es i))))
  | jNewArraySized _ n => addCounts (one 9) (countExpr n)
  | jAnonFn _ _ body =>
    addCounts (one 10) (sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {body.len} (\lam i => countStmt (body i))))
  | jStringLit _ => one 11
  | jIntLit _ => one 12
  | jLongLit _ => one 13

\func countStmt (s : JStmt) : Array Nat \elim s
  | jLocal _ _ _ nothing => one 14
  | jLocal _ _ _ (just e) => addCounts (one 14) (countExpr e)
  | jAssign l r => addCounts (one 15) (addCounts (countExpr l) (countExpr r))
  | jSwitch scrut cs dflt =>
    addCounts (one 16) (addCounts (countExpr scrut) (addCounts
      (sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {cs.len} (\lam i =>
        sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {((cs i).2).len} (\lam j => countStmt ((cs i).2 j))))))
      (sumCounts (mkArray {Array Nat} {dflt.len} (\lam j => countStmt (dflt j))))))
  | jThrow e => addCounts (one 17) (countExpr e)
  | jReturn e => addCounts (one 18) (countExpr e)
  | jExprStmt e => addCounts (one 19) (countExpr e)
  | jComment _ => one 20

-- Outside the mutual cycle, so `map` is fine from here down.
--
-- `jMemberComment` contributes nothing: it is a `JMember`, not a `JStmt`, and
-- `JavaEval` has no clause for it -- the class-body comment an erased inductive
-- produces is not an evaluable thing.
\func countMember (mem : JMember) : Array Nat \elim mem
  | jMethod m => sumCounts (map countStmt m.mBody)
  | jMemberComment _ => zeroCounts

\func countClass (c : JClass) : Array Nat => sumCounts (map countMember c.classMembers)

-- The counts for one λ□ program, compiled exactly as the harness compiles it.
\func countProgram (p : LBProgram) : Array Nat => countClass (compileClass {targetLong} p)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The report.
--
-- STRUCTURED FOR COST, and the cost is not incidental -- the first version of
-- this section did not finish in nine minutes.
--
-- `countProgram` runs `ToJava.compileClass`, which is seconds per program
-- (measured: peano 0.85 s / 78 nodes, leanbench-even 4.5 s / 400 nodes). The
-- obvious shape -- `\let counts => map countProgram ps` and then a
-- `progsWith i counts` / `occsOf i counts` per clause row -- re-evaluates the
-- whole vector at every access, because `map` builds a lazy `DArray` whose `at`
-- is re-invoked. That is 21 rows x 3 references x 7 programs, so roughly 440
-- compilations instead of 7.
--
-- So the whole report is ONE structural fold (`scan`) that visits each program
-- once and returns the three things the table needs already accumulated: the
-- per-program rows, the per-clause totals, and the per-clause presence counts.
-- `coverageTable` destructures it with `\case`, which forces it exactly once.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func nthOr0 (i : Nat) (c : Array Nat) : Nat => \case nthArr i c \with {
  | nothing => 0
  | just v => v
}

-- A count vector reduced to presence: 1 where the clause occurs at all. Summing
-- these across programs gives "how many programs exercise this clause", which is
-- the headline number the plan asks for.
\func signs (xs : Array Nat) : Array Nat \elim xs
  | nil => nil
  | 0 :: xs => 0 :: signs xs
  | suc _ :: xs => 1 :: signs xs

\func spacesStr (n : Nat) : String \elim n
  | 0 => ""
  | suc n => " " ++ spacesStr n

-- The negative case is `neg (suc _)`, not `neg _`: `neg 0` is DEFINITIONALLY
-- `pos 0` (Prelude's constructor condition on `Int`), and Arend rightly rejects
-- a `neg 0` clause returning `s` beside a `pos 0` one returning `s ++ ""` --
-- those are equal only up to a lemma, not by reduction.
\func padRight (width : Nat) (s : String) : String =>
  \case width Nat.- (s.bytes).len \with {
    | pos k => s ++ spacesStr k
    | neg (suc _) => s
  }

\func sumOf (xs : Array Nat) : Nat \elim xs
  | nil => 0
  | x :: xs => x Nat.+ sumOf xs

-- One clause's summary line, read out of the two ALREADY-ACCUMULATED vectors, so
-- nothing is recompiled here.
\func clauseRow (i : Nat) (progs occs : Array Nat) : String =>
  padRight 16 (clauseName i)
    ++ padRight 8 (natToStr (nthOr0 i progs))
    ++ padRight 10 (natToStr (nthOr0 i occs))
    ++ (\case nthOr0 i progs \with {
      | 0 => "  <-- NOTHING EXERCISES THIS"
      | suc _ => ""
    })
    ++ "\n"

-- Rows 0 .. k-1, in index order: `rowsUpTo (suc k) = rowsUpTo k ++ row k`.
\func rowsUpTo (k : Nat) (progs occs : Array Nat) : String \elim k
  | 0 => ""
  | suc k => rowsUpTo k progs occs ++ clauseRow k progs occs

-- The 21 numbers of one program, in clause order.
\func renderVec (xs : Array Nat) : String \elim xs
  | nil => ""
  | x :: xs => padRight 5 (natToStr x) ++ renderVec xs

-- Taking the vector as a PARAMETER is what keeps it to one evaluation: the
-- caller normalizes `countProgram p.2` once and this consumes the result three
-- times without re-running the compiler.
\func combine (name : String) (v : Array Nat)
              (rest : \Sigma String (Array Nat) (Array Nat))
  : \Sigma String (Array Nat) (Array Nat) =>
  ("  " ++ padRight 18 name ++ padRight 7 (natToStr (sumOf v)) ++ renderVec v ++ "\n" ++ rest.1,
   addCounts v rest.2,
   addCounts (signs v) rest.3)

-- (per-program rows, per-clause totals, per-clause presence counts).
\func scan (ps : Array (\Sigma String LBProgram))
  : \Sigma String (Array Nat) (Array Nat) \elim ps
  | nil => ("", zeroCounts, zeroCounts)
  | p :: ps => combine p.1 (countProgram p.2) (scan ps)

\func coverageTable (ps : Array (\Sigma String LBProgram)) : String =>
  \case scan ps \with {
    | (rows, occs, progs) =>
      "JavaEval clause coverage over " ++ natToStr ps.len ++ " corpus program(s).\n" ++
      "STATIC counts -- an upper bound on dynamic coverage; a ZERO is exact.\n" ++
      "See this module's header before quoting these numbers.\n\n" ++
      "per program (nodes, then the 21 clause counts in the order below):\n" ++
      "  " ++ padRight 18 "program" ++ padRight 7 "nodes" ++ "\n" ++
      rows ++ "\n" ++
      padRight 16 "clause" ++ padRight 8 "progs" ++ padRight 10 "occs" ++ "\n" ++
      rowsUpTo nClauses progs occs
  }
